Whether a string has an even number of b's, with a and b the only bytes allowed. The result is a pair: [0 1] if the count is even, [0 0] if it is odd, and [1 "bad character"] for any other byte. The two states, even and odd, are mutually recursive, and each reads the next byte and switches to the other state on a b.
The probe is mutual recursion over a string, with a failure carried as a value through the walk.
---
---
(fn s
  (mutual ((even (fn l
              (if (? l) [0 1]
                  (let c (head l)
                    (if (= c 97) (even (tail l))
                        (if (= c 98) (odd (tail l)) [1 "bad character"]))))))
           (odd (fn l
              (if (? l) [0 0]
                  (let c (head l)
                    (if (= c 97) (odd (tail l))
                        (if (= c 98) (even (tail l)) [1 "bad character"])))))))
    (even s)))
---
"" => [0 1] fuel 200000
"a" => [0 1] fuel 200000
"b" => [0 0] fuel 200000
"abba" => [0 1] fuel 200000
"bab" => [0 1] fuel 200000
"aabab" => [0 1] fuel 200000
"bbb" => [0 0] fuel 200000
"abc" => [1 "bad character"] fuel 200000
"a b" => [1 "bad character"] fuel 200000
